<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened Runtime entitlements for the Developer ID build: DELIBERATELY EMPTY.

  Notarization requires the Hardened Runtime, and the Hardened Runtime blocks
  things by default. Swept needs none of them unblocked. This file exists rather
  than being absent so that the reasoning has a home, and so that a future
  sidecar has somewhere to declare its needs -- not because anything is granted.

  Keep it empty unless a signed build proves otherwise. Every entitlement below
  was considered and rejected on evidence.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
      NOT NEEDED, and this is the one that gets copied in by mistake most
      often. JavaScriptCore's JIT does not run in this process. It runs in
      `com.apple.WebKit.WebContent.xpc`, which:

        * carries `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` itself, and is signed by
          Apple with `TeamIdentifier=not set` -- verified with
          `codesign -d --entitlements :-` against the system framework; and
        * runs with PPID 1. launchd spawns it as an XPC service, so it is not
          forked from us and cannot inherit our signature or entitlements at
          all. Apple's Hardened Runtime documentation grants inheritance only
          to "shared libraries, frameworks, and in-process plug-ins".

      Verified empirically as well as by inspection: a minimal WKWebView app
      signed `--options runtime` with zero entitlements launches, spawns
      WebContent and executes JavaScript. A `mmap(MAP_JIT)` control probe on
      the same machine confirmed the runtime really was denying in-process JIT,
      so that was a live test rather than a vacuous one.

      Electron genuinely does need this, because it bundles V8 and JITs
      in-process. That is where the advice comes from, and it does not transfer
      to a Tauri app which bundles no JS engine (no Contents/Frameworks).

      It would become necessary if Swept ever ships a sidecar that JITs --
      Python/PyInstaller, Node, .NET, a JVM -- or calls JavaScriptCore itself.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      NOT NEEDED, and never alongside allow-jit: Apple's own DTS guidance is
      that including both is pointless, because this one permits MAP_JIT "and
      more". It is strictly broader and blunter. If something ever does need
      JIT, reach for allow-jit, not this.

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      NOT NEEDED. Lets the process load libraries signed by anyone. Swept loads
      no plug-ins and no external binaries. Widely cargo-culted into Tauri
      entitlement files; do not copy it in without a crash log that names
      library validation as the cause.

  com.apple.security.app-sandbox
      IMPOSSIBLE, not merely unnecessary. Swept reads other applications'
      caches, ~/Library/Logs, browser profiles and LaunchAgents. No sandbox
      entitlement makes that possible, which is why this app is not on the Mac
      App Store at all (docs/LEGAL.md). Adding it would break every scanner.

  com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
      NOT NEEDED, for a subtler reason than the others, and the reasoning is
      worth keeping because the obvious reading of the code is wrong.

      Swept DOES cause Apple Events to be sent. The `trash` crate's macOS
      default is `DeleteMethod::Finder`, not NSFileManager -- verified in the
      vendored source of the pinned 5.2.6, where `DeleteMethod::new()` returns
      `Finder` -- and nothing in this repository calls `set_delete_method`. So
      disposal shells out to `osascript` with a `tell application "Finder"`
      script.

      The entitlement is still not needed, because the Hardened Runtime checks
      entitlements per PROCESS, and those events are sent by
      `/usr/bin/osascript` -- a separate, Apple-signed executable that is not
      itself hardened. Adding the entitlement here would gate nothing that is
      currently ungated.

      What those events DO require is TCC consent, attributed to the
      responsible process -- us -- which is why `Info.plist` carries
      `NSAppleEventsUsageDescription`. Without that string the user gets an
      unexplained "Swept wants to control Finder" prompt.

  ASCII only: Apple's documentation says the entitlements file must be
  ASCII-encoded. Nothing here should acquire a smart quote or an em dash.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
